Assistant: The signaling value of central bank communications depends fundamentally on the information content relative to what rational market participants can infer from economic fundamentals. In efficient markets, central bank communications only add value to the extent they reveal private information about the reaction function or future policy intentions not already incorporated in prices. The empirical evidence suggests that while short-term market reactions to communications are substantial, much of this represents noise trading rather than fundamental repricing. Furthermore, excessive forward guidance can create moral hazard by encouraging risk-taking based on perceived policy commitments, while reducing the disciplining effect of market signals on policymakers. Studies examining prediction accuracy show that market-based forecasts often outperform central bank projections, suggesting limited informational advantages. The proliferation of central bank communication may paradoxically reduce signaling value through oversaturation, as demonstrated by diminishing market responses to routine communications over time.

