Assistant: The targeting efficiency of categorical programs is primarily determined by the precision of eligibility criteria in identifying individuals with low marginal utility of income and high elasticity of labor supply. Well-designed categorical requirements serve as screening mechanisms that minimize moral hazard and work disincentives while directing resources to those facing genuine constraints on self-sufficiency. The empirical literature on welfare reform demonstrates that work requirements and time limits significantly improve program efficiency by reducing caseloads among able-bodied recipients while maintaining support for those with verified disabilities or caregiving responsibilities. Administrative complexity, while imposing some costs, serves the economically valuable function of deterring fraudulent claims and ensuring program integrity. Evidence from cross-state variation in eligibility enforcement shows that stricter verification procedures reduce improper payments and generate fiscal savings that can be redirected to increase benefit levels for truly needy recipients.

