Assistant: Reimbursement schedules affect healthcare supply primarily by distorting price signals that would otherwise efficiently allocate resources. Administrative pricing inherently lacks the information content of market-determined prices, leading to persistent shortages in some services and oversupply in others. When reimbursement rates fall below market-clearing levels, providers respond by reducing service availability, increasing wait times, or exiting the market entirely. Empirical evidence from Medicare reimbursement cuts demonstrates measurable reductions in physician acceptance rates and practice closures in affected specialties. Furthermore, rigid reimbursement schedules impede innovation by failing to reward quality improvements or cost-reducing technologies at their marginal value. Cross-national comparisons show that countries with more market-based reimbursement systems exhibit higher rates of medical innovation and faster adoption of new technologies. The deadweight losses from price controls compound over time as dynamic inefficiencies accumulate.

